<Project>

  <!--
    Chain to the repository root's Directory.Build.props first. MSBuild walks up from the project
    directory and stops at the *first* Directory.Build.props it finds, so without this line the
    presence of this file would silently take the four samples out of everything the root one sets:
    net10.0, nullable, TreatWarningsAsErrors, and the BannedApiAnalyzers rule that keeps BCL
    DateTime out of the codebase. That failure would be quiet — the samples would still build.
  -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <!--
    The three properties every sample carries, set once here rather than four times in the project
    files — and set here rather than there for a second reason beyond repetition. None of them is
    about the sample: they are this repository's own bookkeeping, and a reader who copies a sample
    project out of the tree wants the ProjectReference and nothing else. Keeping the machinery in a
    file that does not travel with the copy is the point.

    IsPackable and IsTestProject are the pair benchmarks/DatabentoDotNet.Benchmarks documents at
    length: `dotnet pack` at the root must not produce a package for a sample, and `dotnet test`
    must not try to run an assembly with no tests in it. Both are explicitly false rather than
    merely unset, because Directory.Build.targets reads IsTestProject and an empty value works by
    accident.

    IsSampleProject is read there too and excludes these from $(ShippingProject), and so from #63's
    public API lock. See Directory.Build.targets for why that costs nothing here.

    Between them these are what #66's definition of done means by "excluded from the test run by
    project properties rather than by a filter": the filter argument in ci.yml does not mention
    samples and must not have to.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
    <IsSampleProject>true</IsSampleProject>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Samples are written as one file of top-level statements, so their only type is the compiler's
    own internal Program and there is no public member to document. GenerateDocumentationFile is
    therefore off here where it is on for every shipping project — and turning it on would not add
    a check, only a warning-as-error for a file that has nothing to say.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
